veys converge toward the tied St. Pete Polls and Change Research results, or Democratic outside money arrives late, the race could tighten into lean-R territory.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Ashley Moody (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Appointed Senator Ashley Moody, a former state attorney general, faces state Representative Angie Nixon, a progressive Democrat endorsed by Bernie Sanders, in Florida's special Senate election. Moody holds an overwhelming financial advantage, with roughly $8.5 million on hand to Nixon's $265,000, and outside groups have spent little on either side. Polling since the primary has been mixed: two surveys showed the race tied, but most recent polls put Moody ahead by seven to eleven points, averaging about five to six points statewide.\n\nExpert raters broadly call the race Likely or Solid Republican, and prediction markets give Nixon roughly a ten percent chance. Florida's Republican-leaning electorate and a favorable midterm climate for Democrats cut in opposite directions. Nixon's underfunded campaign would need a substantial late shift to close the gap, while Moody's name recognition and resources make her the clear favorite in the final weeks.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1404, "margin": -5.31}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2259, "margin": -4.8}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1936, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.1439, "margin": -6.5}]}